---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Presentation from "../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

import PotentiometerCircuitDiagram from "../../../assets/courses/foce/img/potentiometer-circuit-diagram.svg";
import RedBoardPinA0 from "../../../assets/courses/foce/img/redboard-rp2350-pin-a0.svg";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `button.value()   # 0 or 1. Nothing else.
pot.read_u16()   # 0, 1, 2, ... all the way to 65535`;
const Figure2 = `pot = ADC(Pin(40))
reading = pot.read_u16()`;
const Figure3 = `# 65536 possible readings, 5 LEDs.
height = reading * (5 + 1) // 65536`;
const Figure4 = `for led in leds:      # every LED, same treatment
    led.value(1)

leds[i].value(1)      # this one, by position`;
const Task1 = `from machine import Pin, ADC
import time

pot = ADC(Pin(40))

while True:
    print(pot.read_u16())
    time.sleep(0.2)
`;
const Task2 = `from machine import Pin, ADC
import time

pot = ADC(Pin(40))

lowest = 65535
highest = 0

while True:
    reading = pot.read_u16()
    if reading < lowest:
        lowest = reading
    if reading > highest:
        highest = reading
    print("now:", reading, "  lowest:", lowest, "  highest:", highest)
    time.sleep(0.2)
`;
const Task3 = `from machine import Pin, ADC
import time

leds = [
    Pin(28, Pin.OUT),
    Pin(29, Pin.OUT),
    Pin(30, Pin.OUT),
    Pin(31, Pin.OUT),
    Pin(32, Pin.OUT),
]
pot = ADC(Pin(40))


def bar_height(reading, led_count):
    return reading * (led_count + 1) // 65536


while True:
    reading = pot.read_u16()
    print("reading:", reading, "  bar:", bar_height(reading, len(leds)))
    time.sleep(0.2)
`;
const Task4 = `from machine import Pin, ADC
import time

leds = [
    Pin(28, Pin.OUT),
    Pin(29, Pin.OUT),
    Pin(30, Pin.OUT),
    Pin(31, Pin.OUT),
    Pin(32, Pin.OUT),
]
pot = ADC(Pin(40))


def bar_height(reading, led_count):
    return reading * (led_count + 1) // 65536


def show_bar(leds, count):
    for i in range(len(leds)):
        if i < count:
            leds[i].value(1)
        else:
            leds[i].value(0)


while True:
    reading = pot.read_u16()
    height = bar_height(reading, len(leds))
    show_bar(leds, height)
    print("reading:", reading, "  bar:", height)
    time.sleep(0.1)
`;
const Output1 = `reading: 148     bar: 0
reading: 11302   bar: 1
reading: 24907   bar: 2
reading: 38455   bar: 3
reading: 51100   bar: 4
reading: 65216   bar: 5`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/foce/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.8: Analog Input</h1>
        <p class="text-sn-theme">The Messy Real World &mdash; Day 1 of 4</p>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 2.8</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Analog Input</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Analog Input</p>
              <p>Scaling a Range</p>
              <p>Position in a List</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Activity 2.3 Told You a Half-Truth</h2>
              <p>
                "A digital pin has exactly two states. No in-between."
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                True &mdash; of <i>digital</i> pins. Today you meet the other
                kind, which reports a whole{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">range</b>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Two Kinds of Answer</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                A button is a yes-or-no question. A knob is a{" "}
                <i>how much</i> question, and the board answers with a number
                from <code>0</code> to <code>65535</code>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Reading an Analog Pin</h2>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                <code>ADC</code> stands for{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">analog-to-digital converter</b>
                &mdash; the circuit inside the board that turns a voltage into
                a number your code can use.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>What the Knob Actually Does</h2>
              <p>
                A potentiometer is a strip of resistive material with a slider
                on it. One end sits at 3.3V, the other at 0V, and the middle
                leg picks off whatever voltage is at the slider's position.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Turn the knob, move the slider, change the voltage. The ADC
                turns that voltage into your number.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Too Big a Number</h2>
              <p>
                65,536 possible readings. Five LEDs. Those do not line up, so
                the reading has to be <b class="text-sn-theme">scaled</b> down
                before it can mean anything to the array.
              </p>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                Six buckets, because "no LEDs" is a valid answer too. Integer
                division from Activity 1.4 does the whole job.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Now You Need Positions</h2>
              <p>
                Every animation in 2.6 treated all five LEDs the same way. A
                bar graph does not &mdash; it lights the <i>first three</i>
                and leaves the rest dark.
              </p>
              <Code code={Figure4} frame="code" lang="python" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">
                <code>for led in leds:</code> cannot express "the first
                three." Asking for <code>leds[i]</code> by{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">position</b> can.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Reading a potentiometer with <code>ADC</code> and <code>read_u16()</code></li>
                <li>Explaining how an analog pin differs from a digital one</li>
                <li>Scaling a 0&ndash;65535 reading down onto a smaller range</li>
                <li>Lighting the first N LEDs to show a value as a bar</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Analog Input</dt>
                  <dd>A pin that reports a range of values rather than just on or off.</dd>
                  <dt>ADC</dt>
                  <dd>Analog-to-digital converter &mdash; the circuit that turns a voltage into a number.</dd>
                  <dt>Potentiometer</dt>
                  <dd>A knob that divides a voltage; its middle leg reports the slider's position.</dd>
                  <dt>Scaling</dt>
                  <dd>Converting a value from one range into another, here 0&ndash;65535 down to 0&ndash;5.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Leave Activity 2.6's five-LED array exactly as it is. Create a new
            MicroPython script named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">2-8-analog-input.py</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Read the Knob</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Wire the potentiometer: its two outer legs to <code>3V3</code> and <code>GND</code>, its middle leg to <code>A0</code>.</li>
            <li>Create it as an <code>ADC</code> and print the reading in a loop.</li>
            <li>Turn the knob slowly and watch the numbers move.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The analog pins are the six on the bottom header, labeled{" "}
            <code>A0</code> through <code>A5</code>. <code>A0</code> is at the
            far right:
          </p>

          <Image src={RedBoardPinA0} alt="The SparkFun IoT RedBoard RP2350 with the pin labeled A0 outlined on the bottom header" width={620} class="my-3" />

          <p>
            It does not matter which outer leg goes to <code>3V3</code> and
            which to <code>GND</code> &mdash; swapping them just reverses which
            way you turn the knob to get a bigger number.
          </p>

          <Image src={PotentiometerCircuitDiagram} alt="Circuit diagram of a potentiometer with its outer legs on 3V3 and GND and its wiper connected to analog pin A0" width={560} class="my-3" />

          <!-- PHOTO: potentiometer wired beside the five-LED array -->
          <div
            class="border border-secondary rounded p-4 my-3 text-center text-secondary"
            style="border-style: dashed !important;"
          >
            [PHOTO PLACEHOLDER &mdash; Task 1: Potentiometer Wired Beside the Five-LED Array]
          </div>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-8-analog-input.py" mark={{ range: "1,4,7" }} />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Find Its Real Range</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Track the lowest and highest readings you have seen, the same way you tracked your best reaction time in 2.7.</li>
            <li>Turn the knob all the way in both directions.</li>
            <li><b>Write down the two numbers you actually get.</b> You will need them next session.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Do not assume the answer. A knob turned fully one way may not read{" "}
            <code>0</code>, and fully the other way may not read{" "}
            <code>65535</code>. Find out what <i>your</i> knob does.
          </p>

          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-8-analog-input.py" mark={{ range: "10-15" }} ins={{ range: "6-7,10-15" }} />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Scale the Reading</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Bring the five-LED array into this script.</li>
            <li>Write <code>bar_height(reading, led_count)</code> that converts a reading into a whole number of LEDs.</li>
            <li>Print the reading and the height side by side, and check the height climbs 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 as you turn.</li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-8-analog-input.py" mark={{ range: "14-15" }} ins={{ range: "4-10,14-15,20" }} />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Light the Bar</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write <code>show_bar(leds, count)</code> that lights the first <code>count</code> LEDs and turns the rest off.</li>
            <li>Call it every time round the loop, so the bar follows the knob live.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            This is the function that needs <code>leds[i]</code>. Work out for
            yourself why <code>for led in leds:</code> cannot do it before you
            read the code below.
          </p>

          <Code code={Task4} frame="code" lang="python" title="2-8-analog-input.py" mark={{ range: "18-23" }} ins={{ range: "18-23,28-31" }} />

          <!-- PHOTO: bar graph mid-range, three LEDs lit -->
          <div
            class="border border-secondary rounded p-4 my-3 text-center text-secondary"
            style="border-style: dashed !important;"
          >
            [PHOTO PLACEHOLDER &mdash; Task 4: Bar Graph at Mid-Range, Three LEDs Lit]
          </div>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): The Knob Sets the Speed</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Bring your <code>chase()</code> function over from Activity 2.6.</li>
            <li>Use the knob to set the pause length instead of the bar height, so turning it speeds the animation up and slows it down.</li>
            <li>A pause between about <code>0.02</code> and <code>0.5</code> seconds works well &mdash; scale the reading into that range.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>Turning the knob from one end to the other should print something like this, and move the bar with it:</p>

          <Code code={Output1} frame="terminal" lang="txt" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <ul>
            <li>The bar follows the knob smoothly, with no jumps of more than one LED</li>
            <li>Fully one way gives no LEDs lit; fully the other gives all five</li>
            <li>The printed height never goes below 0 or above 5</li>
            <li>Holding the knob still leaves the reading roughly &mdash; but perhaps not exactly &mdash; steady</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Activity 2.3 said a digital pin has exactly two states. How is an analog pin different, and why did the reading have to be scaled before the LED array could show it?</li>
            <li>Every animation in 2.6 used <code>for led in leds:</code>, but <code>show_bar()</code> needs <code>leds[i]</code>. Explain what the bar graph asks for that a plain traversal cannot give.</li>
            <li>In Task 2 you recorded your knob's real lowest and highest readings. Were they exactly 0 and 65535? What would go wrong in a program that simply assumed they were?</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
